-- title:
--   Depth floor above p gives the bound 2e(w∣ p)b+1
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a number field whose ring of integers is an algebra over $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$, integral over it, let $\mu : (\mathbb{A}_K)^\times \to \mathbb{C}^\times$ be a multiplicative character of the units of the adele ring of $K$, let $p$ be a height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ and let $N$ be an ideal of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$. Write $\mathrm{primeFibre}\ \mathbb{Q}\ K\ p$ for the set of height-one primes $w$ of $\mathcal{O}_K$ with $w$ lying under $p$, i.e. $w.\mathrm{under}\ \mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}} = p$. Assume the depth hypothesis: for every such $w$,
--   $$4\bigl(\operatorname{count}_w(N\mathcal{O}_K) + n(\psi_w) + 1\bigr) \le a(\mu_w),$$
--   where $\operatorname{count}_w$ is the exponent of $w$ in the fractional ideal generated by the image of $N$ under $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}} \to \mathcal{O}_K$, $n(\psi_w) = \mathrm{addCharLevel}$ of the standard local additive character $\mathrm{psiLocal}\ K\ w$ (the supremum of the integers $n$ such that the character is trivial on all $x$ with $\mathrm{Valued.v}\,x \le \mathrm{WithZero.exp}\,n$), and $a(\mu_w) = \mathrm{conductorExponentAt}$ of the local component $\mathrm{localChar}\ \mu\ w$ of $\mu$ at $w$ (the infimum of the $c$ with $\mu_w$ trivial on $\mathrm{higherUnitsAt}\ K\ w\ c$ and non-trivial on $\mathrm{higherUnitsAt}\ K\ w\ m$ for every $m < c$). Let finally $b$ be a natural number with $p^b \mid N$ and $p^{b+1} \nmid N$. The conclusion is that for every $w$ above $p$,
--   $$2\,e(w\mid p)\,b + 1 \le a(\mu_w),$$
--   with $e(w\mid p) = \mathrm{Ideal.ramificationIdx'}$ of $w$ over the prime beneath it.
--
--   Elementary bookkeeping converting the depth hypothesis in which a twisted local functional equation is available into the shape of floor, $2e(w\mid p)b+1$, at which the local identification of conductor exponents above $p$ is formulated. It is used in the Rankin–Selberg local integral arguments, in particular in the construction of a non-vanishing twisted local integral and in the identification of local zeta integrals with the standard root number.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_LanglandsTunnell_RankinSelberg_two_mul_ramificationIdx_mul_add_one_le_conductorExponentAt_of_depth_floor.lean

